Buy your first lesson, and get your second lesson free. Check out the link in the description below.

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:11.016
Hello, everyone! Welcome to the Langfocus channel, 
and my name is Paul.

00:00:11.016 --> 00:00:13.876
Today, I am going to talk about the Swahili language,

00:00:13.996 --> 00:00:16.636
or "Kiswahili," as it's called in Swahili.

00:00:16.836 --> 00:00:19.336
And by the way, "ki" is a prefix that means "language."

00:00:19.556 --> 00:00:22.616
Swahili is one of the most widely
 spoken languages in Africa;

00:00:22.616 --> 00:00:27.056
and if we only consider languages that are native to Africa,
then, it is the most widely spoken.

00:00:27.316 --> 00:00:30.416
It is widely spoken over a wide area of East Africa.

00:00:30.436 --> 00:00:35.696
The number of native speakers of Swahili is actually 
rather small -- somewhere between 5 and 15 million --

00:00:35.876 --> 00:00:40.836
but it is widely spoken as a lingua franca 
that unites the linguistically diverse population.

00:00:40.996 --> 00:00:46.436
Estimates vary, but the total number of proficient speakers, 
if we include second-language and third-language speakers,

00:00:46.556 --> 00:00:49.476
could be as high as 150 million,  or even more.

00:00:49.556 --> 00:00:55.076
Swahili is an official language in Kenya, Tanzania, Uganda, and the Democratic Republic of the Congo,

00:00:55.196 --> 00:00:57.996
and it's spoken widely in other countries 
like Rwanda and Burundi.

00:00:58.256 --> 00:01:03.676
The closely related Comorian language, spoken in Comoros, 
is also sometimes considered a dialect of Swahili.

00:01:03.856 --> 00:01:07.496
Chances are that you know a little bit of Swahili already, 
whether you know it or not.

00:01:07.536 --> 00:01:10.936
If you've seen the Disney movie "The Lion King", 
then you know the name "Simba".

00:01:11.116 --> 00:01:12.876
"Simba" means "lion" in Swahili.

00:01:13.056 --> 00:01:16.476
And maybe you remember Nala. 
"Nala" means "gift" in Swahili.

00:01:16.616 --> 00:01:20.256
Then, there's Rafiki. 
"Rafiki" means "friend" in Swahili.

00:01:20.376 --> 00:01:23.496
And of course, there's Pumba.
"Pumba" means "stupid" in Swahili.

00:01:23.776 --> 00:01:28.316
And of course, there's "hakuna matata." 
"Hakuna matata" means "no worries" in Swahili.

00:01:28.656 --> 00:01:33.696
Simba, nala, rafiki, pumba, hakuna matata.

00:01:34.556 --> 00:01:36.516
Swahili is one of the Bantu languages.

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:39.776
That's a traditional branch 
of the Niger-Congo language family.

00:01:39.856 --> 00:01:45.016
There are around 250 Bantu languages, depending 
on what we consider a language versus a dialect.

00:01:45.016 --> 00:01:46.696
 

00:01:46.736 --> 00:01:52.896
The history of Swahili is somewhat unclear, 
but it originally developed as a language 
of coastal areas of Kenya and Tanzania.

00:01:53.196 --> 00:02:00.736
Fishermen spread the language to nearby islands, 
then over the following centuries, traders from these islands 
spread the language to a larger area of the coast.

00:02:00.736 --> 00:02:05.176
Today, this coastal region, stretching
from southern Somalia all the way to Mozambique,

00:02:05.256 --> 00:02:07.776
is where most of Swahili's native speakers are found.

00:02:07.836 --> 00:02:14.636
The areas where Swahili was spoken had a lot 
of interaction with foreign traders, particularly those
from the Middle East, throughout the Middle Ages.

00:02:15.016 --> 00:02:22.076
One of the main commodities the traders were seeking 
was cloves. So Persian and Arabic traders established 
cloves farms in the Zanzibar Archipelago.

00:02:22.236 --> 00:02:25.316
And they also established trading settlements,
along the mainland coast.

00:02:25.576 --> 00:02:30.756
With both foreign traders and local African Swahili 
speaking traders settling along the new trade route,

00:02:30.916 --> 00:02:34.296
Swahili absorbed many foreign loanwords, 
especially from Arabic.

00:02:35.056 --> 00:02:38.376
In fact, the name of the language itself "Swahili"
comes from Arabic.

00:02:38.576 --> 00:02:42.356
The Arabic word for coast is "sāHil"
and the plural form is "sawāHil"

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:47.176
So, with the prefix "ki", Kiswahili means 
"the language of the coasts".

00:02:47.436 --> 00:02:54.196
In large part, this interaction between foreign Muslim 
traders and local Africans is what made
the Swahili language what it is today,

00:02:54.356 --> 00:02:59.076
a Bantu language with a large number of
loanwords from other languages, especially Arabic.

00:02:59.196 --> 00:03:01.896
but also Persian, Malay and other languages too.

00:03:02.216 --> 00:03:05.836
Contact with European colonial powers
also influenced Swahili.

00:03:05.976 --> 00:03:13.636
Portugal began establishing colonies in East Africa 
in 1505 CE, including in Zanzibar and 
along the Kenya and Tanzanian coasts.

00:03:14.556 --> 00:03:18.416
And, as you might predict, this led to the adoption 
of some Portuguese vocabulary.

00:03:19.436 --> 00:03:25.976
But, by about 1730 CE, Omani Arabs had retaken that region 
from the Portuguese and re-establish control over it.

00:03:26.196 --> 00:03:32.236
Around that time, in the early 18th century, 
Swahili spread further inland with Arab ivory 
and slave trade caravans.

00:03:32.456 --> 00:03:39.496
This brought Swahili to more inland areas of Kenya and 
Tanzania, to the eastern part of Congo, Northern Uganda 
and Rwanda and Burundi

00:03:39.536 --> 00:03:44.036
In the mid 18th century, the British 
and the Germans began colonizing the area.

00:03:44.136 --> 00:03:48.796
Germany took Tanganyika 
or modern-day Tanzania as a colony and 1886.

00:03:48.796 --> 00:03:54.656
And the British took control of Kenya, 
then called the "East Africa Protectorate" in 1895.

00:03:54.736 --> 00:04:00.976
And they both encourage the use of Swahili as 
a national language to unite the population,
which spoke dozens of different languages

00:04:01.156 --> 00:04:05.036
Germany made Swahili, the official 
and administrative language in Tanganyika,

00:04:05.096 --> 00:04:09.156
while the British made English the official language 
at the highest levels in Kenya.

00:04:09.216 --> 00:04:12.836
English was the language 
for national administration and for higher education,

00:04:12.836 --> 00:04:17.616
but Swahili was made the language for
local administration and for primary education.

00:04:17.616 --> 00:04:21.036
In order to help spread the Swahili language, 
it needed  to be standardized.

00:04:21.036 --> 00:04:27.996
So, in 1928, a conference was called for this purpose
and the dialect of Zanzibar was chosen as the basis 
for the standard language

00:04:28.256 --> 00:04:33.676
Its status as an official language or national language 
as well as the language of education has made Swahili

00:04:33.676 --> 00:04:40.416
widely spoken as a 2nd or 3rd language in Kenya and
Tanzania and in the eastern part of the Dem. Rep. of Congo.

00:04:40.616 --> 00:04:44.776
In Tanzania, around 80% of the people can speak it 
and, amongst the younger generations,

00:04:44.776 --> 00:04:48.956
it is becoming more widely spoken as 
a native language, especially in the urban areas.

00:04:49.156 --> 00:04:51.016
And the situation is similar in Kenya.

00:04:51.196 --> 00:04:55.696
In the Democratic Republic of Congo, it's most widely 
spoken in the eastern part of the country.

00:04:55.776 --> 00:04:59.396
But, in total, around 50% of the population
are proficient in the language.

00:04:59.656 --> 00:05:04.296
It is also fairly widely spoken in Rwanda and Burundi
and to some extent in Uganda as well.

00:05:04.496 --> 00:05:09.496
But, even though Swahili has been made
an official language in Uganda 
and it is compulsory to learn in schools,

00:05:09.496 --> 00:05:13.776
a lot of people are not interested in learning it 
and a lot of schools haven't actually been teaching it.

00:05:13.936 --> 00:05:15.036
At least, that's what I hear.

00:05:15.296 --> 00:05:16.596
So what is Swahili like?

00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:17.816
 

00:05:17.936 --> 00:05:19.936
Well, it used to be written in the Arabic script.

00:05:20.056 --> 00:05:24.036
But, because of European colonial influence,
it is now written in the Latin script.

00:05:24.196 --> 00:05:28.236
And it's written phonetically, so that each letter 
represents just one sound in the language.

00:05:28.696 --> 00:05:29.316
 

00:05:29.676 --> 00:05:33.796
Swahili has five vowel sounds which are
always fully pronounced and not reduced.

00:05:33.996 --> 00:05:41.156
There are no diphthong. So that means that 
there are no combination of vowels pronounced 
as a single syllable like "ai"or "au" or "ou"

00:05:41.316 --> 00:05:45.056
If you see two vowel side by side, 
they have to be pronounced as separate syllables.

00:05:45.256 --> 00:05:51.076
This makes the phonology generally quite easy to learn.
And all Swahili consonants have English equivalents.

00:05:51.196 --> 00:05:53.516
But there are some things to be careful of, too.

00:05:53.736 --> 00:05:58.296
Nasal consonants can come before other consonants, 
with no vowel in between. For example:

00:05:58.636 --> 00:06:00.636
"mtoto" means "child"

00:06:00.716 --> 00:06:02.556
"mbwa" means "dog"

00:06:02.836 --> 00:06:04.836
"ndizi" means "banana"

00:06:05.556 --> 00:06:11.316
Learning how to pronounce these sounds together 
maybe a challenge at first, if you've never spoken 
a language with that feature before.

00:06:11.316 --> 00:06:12.076
 

00:06:12.156 --> 00:06:12.836
 

00:06:13.396 --> 00:06:17.916
There are no articles in Swahili. 
There is no equivalent to "a" or "the" in the language.

00:06:18.056 --> 00:06:18.916
 

00:06:19.336 --> 00:06:21.736
Swahili has a system of noun classes.

00:06:21.856 --> 00:06:26.876
Noun classes are categories of different types 
of nouns that are represented by specific prefixes.

00:06:27.096 --> 00:06:30.696
In most cases, 
one singular and one plural for each type of noun.

00:06:31.396 --> 00:06:33.316
First the "m-wa" class.

00:06:33.616 --> 00:06:36.696
Nouns in this class represent people or animals beings.

00:06:36.916 --> 00:06:41.236
Words with the "m" prefix are singular,
those with the "wa-" prefix are plural.

00:06:41.316 --> 00:06:50.396
For example: 
baby -&gt; mtoto ; babies -&gt; watoto
insect -&gt; mdudu ; insects -&gt; wadudu

00:06:51.616 --> 00:06:56.276
Another class is the "m-mi" class.
Nouns in this class represent trees or plants.

00:06:56.496 --> 00:07:01.556
Words with "m-" or "mu-" prefix are singular 
while those with the "mi-" prefix are plural.

00:07:01.796 --> 00:07:04.836
tree : mti ;  trees -&gt; miti.

00:07:05.676 --> 00:07:07.856
Another class is the "n-n" class.

00:07:08.036 --> 00:07:13.476
This class includes a wide variety of nouns
including some animals, loanwords 
and miscellaneous other words.

00:07:13.576 --> 00:07:17.256
This is the biggest of the noun classes,
because of all the loan words in Swahili.

00:07:17.456 --> 00:07:21.036
And for this class, the "n-" prefix denotes 
both singular and plural.

00:07:21.176 --> 00:07:24.796
Bird : ndege ; Birds : ndege

00:07:24.976 --> 00:07:32.316
But this class gets quite complicated and sometimes, 
the prefix changes. It changes to a "m-" sound 
like an "m" before a "b" or a "v".

00:07:32.776 --> 00:07:36.136
Wine : mvinyo ; Wines : mvinyo

00:07:36.936 --> 00:07:39.796
And other words in this class lose their prefix altogether.

00:07:39.936 --> 00:07:44.976
So it's interesting to note that, in English, the plural form 
is indicated by the "s" suffix, at the end of the word.

00:07:45.016 --> 00:07:48.336
But, in Swahili, it's indicated by a prefix 
at the beginning of the word.

00:07:48.596 --> 00:07:52.756
Noun classes are known to be one of
the biggest challenges facing learners of Swahili.

00:07:52.936 --> 00:07:53.716
 

00:07:54.296 --> 00:07:58.476
Nouns class prefixes are also applied 
to adjectives that modify those nouns.

00:07:58.736 --> 00:08:01.436
So the word for "good". The base form is "-zuri".

00:08:01.796 --> 00:08:09.296
But we add a prefix into. 
"mtu mzuri" means "a good person" 
"watu wazuri" means "good people".

00:08:09.416 --> 00:08:12.916
So the prefix changes not only on the noun,
but also on the adjective.

00:08:13.616 --> 00:08:14.276
 

00:08:14.756 --> 00:08:19.216
Adverbs can be formed from the base form 
of the adjective by adding the prefix v.

00:08:19.496 --> 00:08:22.556
Good : -zuri ; Well : vizuri

00:08:22.776 --> 00:08:26.276
Bad : -baya ; Badly : Vibaya.

00:08:26.336 --> 00:08:31.176
Here's an example sentence : 
"Alisoma vibaya" = "he read badly"

00:08:32.056 --> 00:08:36.076
Adverbs can also be formed from nouns
by adding the word "kwa" before them

00:08:36.276 --> 00:08:40.316
"siri" means "secret" 
"kwa siri" means "secretly".

00:08:40.656 --> 00:08:41.476
 

00:08:41.856 --> 00:08:47.836
Aside from noun classes, the other main thing about 
Swahili that require some adjustment from learners 
is the verbal system.

00:08:47.836 --> 00:08:57.216
In Swahili, a basic verb consists of a subject prefix, 
a tense marker, an object infix, if the object is not a separate word, and the verb stem.

00:08:57.436 --> 00:08:58.576
Here are some examples.

00:08:58.836 --> 00:09:02.096
"Alinipa kitabu". This means "he gave me the book".

00:09:02.216 --> 00:09:07.256
"a" is the subject pronoun meaning "he" .
"li" is the tense marker indicating past tense.

00:09:07.416 --> 00:09:12.136
"ni" is the object marker for "me" 
and "pa" is the verb stem meaning "give"

00:09:12.396 --> 00:09:15.456
And "kitabu" it means "book",
and that's a loanword from Arabic.

00:09:15.636 --> 00:09:18.656
We can change the tense of the sentence,
by changing the tense marker.

00:09:18.856 --> 00:09:23.176
"..." So this means "he will give me the book", 
in the future tense.

00:09:23.376 --> 00:09:27.476
So we changed "li", the past tense marker,
to "ta", the future tense marker.

00:09:27.576 --> 00:09:33.156
Now let's change the subject of the sentence. 
"...." that means "you will give me the book".

00:09:33.616 --> 00:09:38.736
So we changed the subject prefix from 
"a", which means "he", to "u", which means "you".

00:09:39.396 --> 00:09:42.436
One interesting thing is that 
there are negative subject prefixes.

00:09:42.476 --> 00:09:46.796
That means we can make the sentence negative 
by using a different subject pronoun.

00:09:46.916 --> 00:09:51.476
Here's an example : "Hatanipa kitabu"
That means "he will not give me the book".

00:09:51.716 --> 00:09:56.256
So remember the positive form was "atanipa kitabu" 
that meant "he will give me the book"

00:09:56.536 --> 00:10:01.316
but we change the "a" to "ha"
and that is the negative equivalent of the subject prefix.

00:10:01.516 --> 00:10:07.796
So every subject prefix has a negative equivalent 
and you have to remember both and you have to think 
about which one to use when you're speaking.

00:10:08.036 --> 00:10:12.176
The basic syntax of Swahili is SVO, 
when there is no object infix.

00:10:14.476 --> 00:10:16.516
This means "Elephants eat grass".

00:10:16.756 --> 00:10:21.496
In the sentence, "tembo" means "Elephantz";
"wa" equals "they", that's the subject prefix;

00:10:21.516 --> 00:10:26.776
"na" equals present tense marker;
"kula" equals "eat" and "nyasi" equals grass.

00:10:27.036 --> 00:10:32.896
So you can see that, when we use a specific noun as 
the subject, we still use the subject prefix attached to the verb.

00:10:33.076 --> 00:10:36.936
Another example "Ninapenda kula" 
This means "I love eating"

00:10:37.056 --> 00:10:43.616
"Ni" is the subject marker meaning "I"
"na" is the present tense marker 
"penda" equals "love" and "kula" equals "eating".

00:10:43.796 --> 00:10:46.136
And of course, that's SVO 
(Rem : SVO = subject-verb-object)

00:10:46.336 --> 00:10:47.776
Is Swahili easy to learn?

00:10:47.936 --> 00:10:50.716
Well, the pronunciation is known for 
being very straightforward.

00:10:50.716 --> 00:10:55.916
And the grammar is very logical but it's also very different,
unless you've studied a Bantu language before.

00:10:56.036 --> 00:11:01.196
The noun class system and the verbal system with 
all of its affixes require some extra attention to learn.

00:11:01.536 --> 00:11:05.976
And, for English speakers, the lack of immediately
recognizable vocabulary might be a challenge.

00:11:06.056 --> 00:11:11.156
But, if you happen to speak Arabic, then you will 
immediately recognize lots of Swahili vocabulary.

00:11:11.276 --> 00:11:16.136
Here are some examples: 
"hatari" comes from Arabic KhaTar, which means danger

00:11:16.136 --> 00:11:19.776
"safari" comes from Arabic "safar" which means "travel"

00:11:19.776 --> 00:11:23.016
"mahali" comes from Arabic "maHal" which means "place".

00:11:23.036 --> 00:11:26.516
"kitabu" comes from the arabic "kitab" which means "book".

00:11:26.616 --> 00:11:30.096
"baridi" comes from the Arabic "baarid" which means "cold".

00:11:30.436 --> 00:11:34.076
And that's just a small sample. 
There are lots of Arabic words in Swahili.

00:11:34.176 --> 00:11:38.376
Although the pronunciation has adapted
to fit into the Swahili phonological system.

00:11:38.376 --> 00:11:40.136
 

00:11:40.136 --> 00:11:43.776
Swahili is a unique product 
of the history of the East African coast

00:11:43.796 --> 00:11:48.956
and the interaction of the local Bantu people 
with foreign traders from the Middle East and elsewhere.

00:11:49.156 --> 00:11:53.856
It is becoming more widely spoken and
continues to grow as a lingua franca in East Africa.

00:11:53.856 --> 00:11:56.116
If you're planning on living or traveling in the region

00:11:56.116 --> 00:12:01.536
or if you just want to learn about Africa and some of 
its cultures, then Swahili would be a great language to learn.

00:12:01.536 --> 00:12:04.416
So the question of the day :
for people who have studied Swahili:

00:12:04.416 --> 00:12:08.756
What did you find challenging about it?
And what did you find straightforward or simple about it?

00:12:08.936 --> 00:12:11.016
and for proficient speakers of Swahili:

00:12:11.176 --> 00:12:13.476
how widely spoken is Swahili in your area ?

00:12:13.616 --> 00:12:17.996
Is it mostly spoken as a second or third language?
Or are there lots of native speakers as well?

00:12:18.236 --> 00:12:20.136
Let us know in the comments down below.

